# Go tests

Use **table-driven** tests (`[]struct{ name string; ... }` + `t.Run(tt.name, ...)`).

```go
func TestFoo(t *testing.T) {
	tests := []struct {
		name    string
		in      string
		want    string
		wantErr bool
	}{
		{name: "ok", in: "a", want: "A"},
		{name: "empty", in: "", wantErr: true},
	}
	for _, tt := range tests {
		t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			got, err := Foo(tt.in)
			if tt.wantErr {
				if err == nil {
					t.Fatal("expected error")
				}
				return
			}
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			if got != tt.want {
				t.Fatalf("got %q want %q", got, tt.want)
			}
		})
	}
}
```

- One `TestX` per behavior family, not `TestXSuccess` / `TestXEmpty` / `TestXError` as separate functions unless the setup cannot share a table.
- Name rows so `go test -v` reads as a spec.
- Keep `t.Helper()` on shared marshal/setup funcs.
